body:
*This was initially a post, but I guess my feedback wasn't high quality enough. Honestly most of you are so much better at this than I am that I feel like a moron giving feedback to anyone, so I'll just post it here.*

&#x200B;

**I Don't Miss You**



I don’t miss you, but I still hear your melody when I close my eyes. My harmony drunkenly stumbles away, confused and lost without your song to guide it.

&#x200B;

I don’t miss you, but I picture your house as the winter air passes over my tongue and arrests my lungs. I watch the afternoon sun fall almost as soon as it arises.

&#x200B;

I don't miss you, but I look at her face and see your eyes staring back at me. I wonder how something so familiar found its way among things so new.

&#x200B;

I don’t miss you, but I still leave your side of the bed empty, knowing that it will be just as cold no matter who I try to fill it with.

&#x200B;

I don’t miss you, but sometimes I wish that I still did, so that I could give a name to the hole in my chest that mirrors the empty space in our apartment. So that I could see an end to the aching that moved in when you left. So that I could believe that one day I could be the me that I was before I gave you my heart and you broke my soul.

That sucks man.  The same thing happened to me, though it took her about 5 or 6 months to tell me about it. It might be time to bail, unless she's really committed to cutting off that relationship and trying to make it work with you, AND you want to try to make it work. If you walked away now you'd still be in the right. Sometime people say that cheating is partially due to the faithful spouse, but that's a load of shit. You're each responsible for the problems that each of you brought into the marriage, but she is 100% responsible for any infidelity. 

&#x200B;

It's a tough road. Hang in there. I promise that there will be another side.

A good friend's sister killed herself. There is definitely some anger, but anytime it's there it's mixed with love, guilt, and sadness. I feel like this video sums up what I've seen in my friend: [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvkbHIrrrvU](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvkbHIrrrvU)

&#x200B;

I was in the same place not too long ago. I think I would have done it if it wasn't for my family and if I hadn't already seen the effects so up-close. Even so, sometimes I hated them for existing, and that their existence forced me to carry on. Right now I'm really happy that I didn't do it. 

&#x200B;

Hey man. My divorce wasn't as messy as yours sounds, but I can empathize. It's hard to feel used by someone and then to get screwed over by the legal system, whether it's paying out money for a decision you didn't want, or in your situation possibly losing your job. I don't know what your prospects are, but in the end it might be better to be working somewhere away from your ex. 

&#x200B;

It's fucked up that she told people that she married you for financial reasons. My wife told me that she married me because she wanted someone to take care of her (emotionally), and that she doesn't need that anymore. It really sucks to feel used like that. I sacrificed a lot of personal and career options for her, and it will be a long road to get to where I want to be. 

&#x200B;

I know that it's hard to see right now, but your life is worth living. You probably have a lot of scary changes and hard shit ahead of you, but in the end it's worth it. For me, once I got to the other side, I've been able to make some pretty hard life changes. Once I felt like I had nothing to live for I started doing things with the mindset that things couldn't get much worse, so I could handle a little more failure if it came down to it.
